# Line-ending policy
#
# Silences the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings that core.autocrlf=true
# fires on Windows when files are committed with LF endings. With explicit
# `eol=lf` here, git stores text files as LF in the repo and checks them out
# as LF on every platform, so working-copy and repo endings always agree --
# no normalization, no warning. .NET tooling, MSBuild, Visual Studio, and
# all modern Windows editors handle LF fine.
#
# To re-align existing tracked files with this policy in a single sweep:
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "Normalize line endings via .gitattributes"

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shell scripts: some older `cmd.exe` consumers require CRLF.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell scripts must always be LF (already the default, pinned
# explicitly so a future addition can't accidentally CRLF-ify them).
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Binary file types -- skip text detection / line-ending conversion entirely.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.ico       binary
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.lib       binary
*.exp       binary
*.pdb       binary
*.obj       binary
*.sqlite    binary
*.zip       binary
*.7z        binary
*.tar       binary
*.gz        binary
